title: Baptist Names in History
description: The many names worn by the people of God from Antioch onward, such as Novatianists and Cathari, and the one conviction beneath each: a church is a company of the saved, baptized upon a profession of faith.
output: lessons/history/baptist-names-in-history.html

# Rebuilt from the original page, section for section. Each name card is a panel with a tap-to-read verse.
# The historical claims (which groups count as Baptist-like, the "succession" trail) are the author's reading and are kept as written.

=== Introduction
--- Baptist Names in History [title]
overline: Adult Sunday School · The Baptist Succession
heading: Baptist Names in History
sub: A study of the many names worn by the people of God through the long centuries between the apostles and today, and the one truth that held beneath every name.
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

--- A name is usually given
lead: Religious bodies rarely choose their own names. A name is usually given by a leader, a town, a doctrine, or an enemy’s sneer.
p: Yet trace the names back far enough, and the same conviction keeps appearing: a church is a company of the saved, baptized upon a profession of faith.
big: Acts 11:26 | mark: the disciples were called Christians first in Antioch

=== Part One · First to Third Century
--- From Antioch to the Novatianists
kicker: Part One · First to Third Century
sub: For nearly three hundred years, one name sufficed. Then the church’s purity began to be tested from within.
panel: Christians | The earliest and simplest name, given at Antioch to the followers of Christ. [[Acts 11:26]]
panel: Novatianists | Followers of Novatian, who withdrew from a church he judged too lax toward those who had denied Christ under persecution, contending for a pure, regenerate membership. [[1 John 2:19]]
panel: Puritans | A name applied to the same separatist conviction, a church cleansed of unconverted membership, not merely reformed in ritual. [[2 Corinthians 6:17]]
panel: Cathari, “The Pure Ones” | Greek for “the pure,” this name traveled alongside Novatianists and Puritans in the third century and would resurface centuries later among the Albigenses, always marking the same insistence on an unmixed, holy church. [[Ephesians 5:26-27]]

=== Part Two · The Schism Within
--- Traditores and Apostates
kicker: Part Two · The Schism Within
heading: Traditores, Infant Baptism, and a State Church
sub: The deepest wound to the early church did not come from Rome’s sword alone. It came from compromise inside the church, and was then sealed into law by an emperor’s embrace.
panel: The Traditores | During the Diocletian persecution, some clergy surrendered copies of Scripture to be burned rather than suffer, earning the name “traditores,” those who handed the Word over. When restored to office afterward without repentance, faithful believers separated from them. [[1 John 2:19]]
panel: Apostates | Others recanted the faith outright under threat of death, then sought readmission to church leadership without discipline, the very compromise that Novatian and his followers refused to accept, at the cost of being cast out themselves. [[2 Thessalonians 2:3]]

--- Infant Baptism and the State Church
kicker: Part Two · The Schism Within
panel: Infant Baptism Takes Root | Alongside this compromise, the doctrine of baptismal regeneration spread, and with it the practice of baptizing infants, formalized as unofficial custom in the third century and later established by imperial law in 416 A.D. [[Mark 16:16]]
panel: Constantine and the State Church | In 313 A.D. the pagan emperor Constantine ended the persecution, but what followed was no less costly. Church and state were fused into one institution, membership by citizenship replaced membership by new birth, and a hierarchy grew up to govern it. [[John 18:36]]

--- Persecution Redirected
kicker: Part Two · The Schism Within
panel-warn: Persecution Redirected | Persecution did not end with Constantine, it changed direction. The sword that had once fallen on all Christians now fell on those who refused the new state church and its infant baptism: Novatianists, Donatists, and every group after them that kept to New Testament faith and practice. [[Acts 5:29]]
xr: Revelation 17:1-2 | A church joined to civil power, pictured in judgment
xr: Galatians 1:6-7 | A warning against a gospel reshaped by compromise

=== Part Three · Third to Seventh Century
--- Donatists and Anabaptists Arise
kicker: Part Three · Third to Seventh Century
sub: Out of the traditores controversy and the new state church, dissenting bodies multiplied, each still contending for a regenerate church and believer’s baptism, and each paying for it in blood.
panel: Donatists | North African believers who refused fellowship with a state-favored church they considered compromised, insisting the church must remain pure. [[Romans 16:17]]
panel: Anabaptists | Literally “re-baptizers,” a name of derision given because they baptized believers who had only received infant baptism, insisting baptism must follow a profession of faith. [[Acts 8:36-38]]
xr: Matthew 3:8 | Baptism follows repentance, never precedes it
xr: Colossians 2:12 | Baptism as burial, requiring prior faith

=== Part Four · Seventh to Eleventh Century
--- The Paulicians and Their Heirs
kicker: Part Four · Seventh to Eleventh Century
sub: Through the Dark Ages, dissenting churches persisted in Armenia, Bulgaria, and Italy, hunted, scattered, and renamed with every migration.
panel: Paulicians | A dissenting movement across Armenia and Asia Minor, later driven into the Balkans and Italy, planting seed that would surface again as new names in new lands. [[Hebrews 11:37-38]]
panel: Petrobrusians | Followers of Peter of Bruys in southern France, who rejected infant baptism and the veneration of the cross. [[1 Peter 4:14]]
panel: Henricians & Arnoldists | Named for Henry of Lausanne and Arnold of Brescia, both of whom preached against clerical corruption and for a church gathered by conversion. [[Galatians 1:8]]

=== Part Five · Eleventh to Sixteenth Century
--- Albigenses, Waldenses, and Lollards
kicker: Part Five · Eleventh to Sixteenth Century
sub: By the eleventh century the names multiply again, a sign not of confusion, but of a movement too widespread to be governed by any single hierarchy.
panel: Albigenses | Named for the town of Albi in southern France, the target of a papal crusade in the thirteenth century for their rejection of Rome’s authority. [[Revelation 2:9-10]]
panel: Vaudois & Waldenses | “People of the valley,” followers of Peter Waldo who preserved vernacular Scripture and sent missionary preachers across Europe from their Alpine strongholds. [[Matthew 5:10-12]]
panel: Lollards | Followers of Wycliffe’s teaching in England, translating and spreading Scripture in the common tongue at great personal cost. [[John 8:31-32]]

=== Part Six · The Unbroken Thread
--- One Way, Many Names
kicker: Part Six · The Unbroken Thread
sub: Lay the centuries side by side, and a single line runs beneath every name: churches gathered by conversion and baptism, never by birth or decree.
step: 100–300 | Christians · Asia Minor, Italy, England, Africa
step: 300–600 | Traditores fall away · Constantine’s state church, 313 · Novatianists · Donatists · Ana-Baptists · Spain, France, Wales, Armenia
step: 600–900 | Paulicians · Ana-Baptists · Armenia, Italy, Bulgaria
step: 900–1300 | Petrobrusians · Henricians · Arnoldists · Albigenses · Ana-Baptists · France, Wales, Germany
step: 1300–1600 | Waldenses · Lollards · Baptists · Holland, France, England
step: 1600–present | John Clarke · Roger Williams · Sandy Creek · Baptists · England, Russia, America
xr: Matthew 16:18 | Christ’s promise that His church would never be extinguished
xr: Matthew 28:20 | A promise of perpetuity to the local church

=== Part Seven · The Mark That Never Changed
--- The Nature of the Church
kicker: Part Seven · The Mark That Never Changed
sub: Every name in this study points back to one contention, argued in every century against every pressure to abandon it.
quote: The nature of the church has been the most distinguished mark of difference between all of our ancestors and the Roman Catholic Church. The Montanists, Novatianists, Donatists, Paulicians, Petrobrusians, Henricians, Arnoldists, Albigenses, Waldenses and all of our Baptist ancestors have contended for the purity of the church. To them, a church was a group of saved people, baptized upon a profession of faith. Since infants can make no profession of faith, infant baptism was rejected. True Baptists today believe the same thing. The truth of the Lord’s church is a great truth with far reaching effects. | Hisel

--- Three Marks
kicker: Part Seven · The Mark That Never Changed
panel: Mark One · A Regenerate Membership | A church is composed of saved people only, never a mixed body of the converted and unconverted gathered by birth or geography.
panel: Mark Two · Believer’s Baptism | Baptism follows a profession of faith; since an infant can profess nothing, infant baptism was rejected in every century by every name.
panel: Mark Three · Separation from the State Church | From Donatist to Waldensian to Baptist, this conviction repeatedly meant standing apart from, and often suffering under, a church allied with civil power.
xr: 1 Timothy 3:15 | The church as the pillar and ground of the truth
xr: Acts 2:41 | The apostolic pattern, receiving the word, then baptism

=== Closing Thought
--- One Church, Called Out [title]
overline: Closing Thought
heading: One Church, Called Out
lead: *Ekklesia*, a called-out assembly. Whatever the century called them, and whatever the world called them in scorn, they held the same truth we hold: a visible, local, regenerate, baptized church, standing on the sole authority of Scripture.
take: Sole Authority of the Scriptures
